Interesting! What's contained in the DOM dump:
- `<cg-auto-pieces></cg-auto-pieces>` — an empty custom element.
- `<coords class="squares rank8 black left">` — a coords element that has a "squares" class! In other words, "on-square coordinates" are rendered as `<coords class="squares ...">`.

The word "square" appears only in the "on-square" board's coord class (boards 4 and 5 have coordinatesOnSquares: true), plus in body text ("coordinates on-square").

There are no `<square>` elements in the dump. That means in this version of chessground, `<square>` DOM elements are not generated in the initial render. Why? Look at render.ts: the desiredSquares loop (lines 100-116) creates square elements… but wait — the loop at line 88, `isSquareNode(el)`, walks through `boardEl.children` looking for existing square nodes. On the first render there's nothing. Next, `for (const [sk, className] of desiredSquares)` — if desiredSquares is empty, nothing gets created!

desiredSquares comes from computeSquareClasses(s) (lines 227-256). Let me read this — maybe it only adds squares that have a non-empty class (special squares: check, last move, selected, etc.)! If computeSquareClasses only returns squares with a special class, then on a new default board (no selection, no last move, no check) desiredSquares would be empty → zero `<square>` elements!

So in this version, the board's 64 tiles are drawn by CSS (the `cg-board` element is styled with a repeating gradient — `background-color: #bfcfdd` + the theme CSS draws the grid). `<square>` elements only exist for special highlights. This is a legitimate rendering optimization — actually, wait, is this really true for upstream chessground? In upstream, all 64 squares exist as `<square>` elements from the start (they carry the dark/light class: `square-00`… hmm, actually, in the upstream code, `squareClass()` returns '' for a normal square, and addSquare adds `klass` only when… let me recall upstream's computeSquareClasses:

Upstream (recent version):
```ts
function computeSquareClasses(s: State): cg.SquareClasses {
  const squares: cg.SquareClasses = new Map();
  for (const k of allKeys) {
    let klass = 'square-' + k;
    if (k === s.selected) klass += ' selected';
    ...
  }
}
```
Hmm, upstream has a `square-XX` class on every square (used by the cburnett theme? no…). Actually, I recall upstream having exactly this: each square's class = "square-00" etc.? Let me check this repo's computeSquareClasses — read lines 227-262.

Also, cburnett.css has selectors like `square-07`… my earlier grep for `^square ` / `square-5` against the CSS found nothing (per the history notes: "the grep on cburnett.css found nothing matching ^square / square-5"). So in this repo, the theme CSS doesn't style square elements per-square — the board's grid must come from elsewhere (probably the base CSS draws the squares with a CSS gradient on cg-board).

Let me check chessground.base.css for the board grid, and read computeSquareClasses.

